<section
  id='case-portfolio-design'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Katja Dev Portfolio — Web &amp; Interaction Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='UX/UI and interaction design case study for a creative portfolio, focused on project discovery, motion, responsive layouts, and a flexible visual identity.'
  data-case='portfolio'
  aria-labelledby='case-portfolio-design-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web &amp; Interaction Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-portfolio-design-title'>Katja Dev Portfolio</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A personal portfolio that brings my design background and front-end work together in one
          clear, creative experience.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI, interaction design, and art direction</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Figma, responsive prototyping, motion direction, design system</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Evolving portfolio identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='portfolio-design'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Katja Dev Portfolio sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='portfolio'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio needed to represent both my visual design experience and my front-end
        development skills. Employers should be able to understand what I do, find relevant
        projects, and move between design and development work without feeling that they are looking
        at two separate portfolios. At the same time, I wanted the website to feel personal and show
        the kind of creative interfaces I enjoy making.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            A creative portfolio needs enough personality to be memorable, but the interface should
            not compete with the projects. The main challenge was finding a balance between
            expressive visuals, animation, and practical navigation. The website also needed to
            present several types of work without making visitors search through one large, mixed
            collection.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Connect development, interaction design, and graphic design within one portfolio
            </li>
            <li>
              Use animation without making longer pages distracting or tiring
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep project discovery clear across screen sizes and colour themes
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-portfoliomoodboard-600.webp'
            data-src='design-portfoliomoodboard-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='515'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio mood board showing colours, typography, water, and movement references'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Mood board exploring water, adaptability, and creative technology
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I divided the work into development, web and interaction design, and graphic design.
              This lets visitors focus on the area that is most relevant to them while still seeing
              the wider range of my experience.
            </li>
            <li>
              I used water and movement as the starting point for the visual identity. Organic
              shapes, wave lines, and fluid transitions express the idea without relying on literal
              water illustrations.
            </li>
            <li>
              Large headings and animated details give the main sections their personality.
              Case-study pages are calmer because they contain more information and need to remain
              comfortable to read.
            </li>
            <li>
              Light and dark themes use the same structure but adjust contrast, colour, and
              illustration details. This keeps both versions connected instead of treating the dark
              theme as an inverted copy.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-ktportfolio-600.webp'
            data-src='design-ktportfolio-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='503'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio wireframes showing the planned page structure and content flow'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Wireframes used to plan the sections and project flow before adding visual styling
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio now presents my design and development work as connected skills while still
        giving each area its own space. The structure can also grow as I add stronger projects and
        remove older work. Designing it taught me to be more selective with animation. A few
        well-placed interactions make more of an impression than adding movement to every section,
        especially on pages where the projects should receive most of the attention.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>